<FieldNote title="Finding a document isn’t the same as knowing you can use it." description="The file is there. The harder questions are whether it’s current, approved and right for this job." number="01" readTime="4 minute read" nextHref="/field-notes/what-should-ai-handle/" nextTitle="What should AI handle—and what should a person decide?">
  <div class="fn-callout"><span class="fn-label">The useful distinction</span><p>Search helps you find information. A dependable working process also tells you which information to act on.</p></div>
  <h2>Three files. One familiar problem.</h2>
  <p>Imagine a project lead preparing tomorrow’s work. An email contains the approved scope. A shared folder has a newer draft. Meeting notes record a client request that may change both. Search can return all three quickly. It still leaves someone to reconstruct what was agreed.</p>
  <figure><div class="fn-flow"><div class="fn-step"><strong>Approved scope</strong><span>Version 2<br />The agreed starting point</span></div><div class="fn-step"><strong>New client request</strong><span>Meeting note<br />A possible change</span></div><div class="fn-step"><strong>Updated draft</strong><span>Version 3 · Awaiting review<br />Not yet the agreed scope</span></div></div><figcaption>Illustrative example: a newer file can exist without replacing the version approved for the work.</figcaption></figure>
  <h2>Make four answers easy to find.</h2>
  <ol><li><strong>What is it for?</strong> Identify the project, task or decision the document supports. A familiar title is not enough when several jobs use the same template.</li><li><strong>What state is it in?</strong> Distinguish a draft from an approved version and an old version kept for reference. Show who approved it, when and for what use.</li><li><strong>What supports it?</strong> Link the client request, source material or requirement behind a consequential statement. A summary should leave a path back to the evidence.</li><li><strong>What changed?</strong> Explain the difference from the previous agreed version and identify the work that needs another look.</li></ol>
  <p>These are established concerns in document management. Microsoft’s documentation, for example, treats version history and content approval as separate controls: saving another version and approving it for publication serve different purposes. <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/libraries/plan-document-versioning-content-approval-and-check-out-controls-in-sharepoint">Read Microsoft’s explanation.</a></p>
  <h2>Start with one handoff.</h2>
  <p>Choose a document your team regularly asks someone to confirm: a scope, instruction or customer record. Put a link to its agreed location in the task where it is used. Make its status, owner and relevant source visible there. Then ask the next person to explain what they would use and why.</p>
  <p>If they still have to message the same colleague, the missing piece may be the decision around the document. Adding more folders won’t answer that question.</p>
  <div class="fn-callout"><span class="fn-label">Try this with your team</span><p>“If the person who prepared this were away tomorrow, could we tell which version to use, what changed and who can resolve a question?”</p></div>
  <h2>Where software can help.</h2>
  <p>Software can bring related records together, retain versions and flag a change for review. AI can help locate candidate sources and prepare a comparison. The reviewer still needs access to the originals and enough context to decide whether the change applies.</p>
  <p>That distinction shapes our approach to Hyphos: connect information to the work, retain the reasoning and make unresolved questions visible. We are developing the product with design partners; this example describes the approach, rather than a claim that every workflow is already available.</p>
  <details><summary>About this note and its source</summary><div><p>This is a practical Hyphos guide informed by our research into document control and knowledge management. The scenario is fictional. The four questions are our suggested starting point, not a certification checklist.</p><p>The linked Microsoft guidance explains versioning and approval in SharePoint Server. It supports the distinction between those controls, rather than a claim about Hyphos implementation.</p></div></details>
</FieldNote>
